INSERT INTO `tblquestion` VALUES (9,7,'Define ˜computational thinking (1 Mark)','A strong answer explains that computational thinking is a logical approach to problem solving which breaks problems into manageable parts so they can be expressed as algorithms and later coded. Excellent responses note it focuses on decomposition, pattern recognition, abstraction and algorithmic design.','[]',1),(10,7,'What is the purpose of decomposition in problem solving? (1 Mark)','High-quality answers recognise that decomposition means breaking a complex problem into smaller, more manageable sub-problems so that each can be solved individually and later combined into a complete solution. Weaker answers may only restate ˜splitting it up™ without explaining the reasoning.','[]',2),(11,7,'Explain the difference between pattern recognition and abstraction in computational thinking. (2 Marks)','A strong response contrasts pattern recognition (spotting similarities or trends across problems) with abstraction (filtering out unnecessary detail to focus on what matters). A well-developed answer may illustrate with an example such as recognising repeated input validation patterns in Python before designing a reusable function.','[]',3),(12,7,'Describe how iteration and selection are used to control program flow in Python. (2 marks)','Excellent answers show understanding that selection (if/elif/else) chooses which path the code follows, while iteration (for/while loops) repeats actions until a condition is met. Stronger students might mention practical examples such as a while-loop prompting for valid input until correct data is entered.\n','[]',4),(13,7,'Using Python, write and explain an algorithm that finds the largest number in a list. (4 Marks)','High-quality answers design or code a loop comparing elements to a stored maximum (e.g. `max_num = numbers[0]; for n in numbers: if n > max_num: max_num = n`). Explanations should reference iteration, comparison and variable updating. Excellent reasoning comments on efficiency or alternative methods using Python™s built-in `max()`.','[]',5),(14,7,'Explain the difference between a linear and binary search algorithm and when each should be used. (4 Marks)','Strong answers identify that linear search checks each element sequentially, whereas binary search repeatedly divides a *sorted* list in half. Excellent reasoning explains that binary search is faster (logarithmic time) but only works on ordered data; students may cite Big-O concepts informally (˜fewer steps as data grows™).','[]',6),(15,7,'Discuss how data type conversion functions support robust code in Python. (5 Marks)','An excellent response links conversions (`int()`, `float()`, `str()`, `bool()`) to preventing type errors and ensuring calculations behave correctly. Reasoned examples show converting user input from `input()` to integers before arithmetic. Strong answers mention that validation complements conversion and that misuse can still raise exceptions.\n','[]',7),(16,7,'Analyse why naming conventions and code style matter for readability and maintenance. (5 Marks)','Top-level responses explain that consistent styles (camelCase, snake_case, whitespace, commenting) make code easier for teams to debug and extend. A strong analytical answer connects readability to fewer logical errors and better collaboration. Weaker answers may only list conventions without reasoning.','[]',8),(17,7,'Evaluate the advantages and disadvantages of using pre-written libraries in software development. (6 Marks)','A well-developed answer evaluates benefits (reduced coding time, reliability, community testing) against drawbacks (dependency risk, version conflicts, reduced learning of core logic). Excellent reasoning references examples such as using `matplotlib` or `pandas` and judging suitability for project goals.','[]',9),(18,7,'Explain how validation techniques improve data integrity, with Python examples. (6 Marks)','High-quality responses explain presence, length, range, type and format checks, showing how these prevent invalid data entry. Example: using `if 0 <= age <= 120:` to validate. Excellent answers link validation to user experience and note that validation reduces runtime errors but must be balanced to avoid rejecting valid inputs.','[]',10),(19,7,'Design and justify a testing plan for a Python program that reads a file of student scores and outputs grades.','An excellent answer describes planned tests (unit, boundary, integration) with valid/invalid test data, expected results, and rationale. Strong reasoning links each test to verifying functionality such as file handling and grade boundaries. Advanced responses discuss automation or regression testing benefits.','[]',11),(20,7,'Design and justify a testing plan for a Python program that reads a file of student scores and outputs grades. (8 Marks)','An excellent answer describes planned tests (unit, boundary, integration) with valid/invalid test data, expected results, and rationale. Strong reasoning links each test to verifying functionality such as file handling and grade boundaries. Advanced responses discuss automation or regression testing benefits.','[]',12),(21,7,'<p>Define digital inclusion and explain why it is important in software design. (2 Marks)</p>','High-quality answers define digital inclusion as ensuring all users can access and benefit from digital services regardless of ability, age, or resources. Stronger responses mention accessibility standards or removing bias in datasets; weaker ones might only mention ˜making it fair™.','[]',13),(22,7,'Explain how emerging technologies such as AI and IoT are changing software development. (4 Marks)','Excellent answers link AI to automation and predictive systems, IoT to data collection and remote control. Well-developed reasoning considers benefits (efficiency, innovation) and drawbacks (privacy, security). Top answers evaluate how developers must adapt coding practices for real-time and distributed systems.','[]',14),(23,7,'Analyse the impact of increased automation on organisational culture and society. (6 Marks)','A comprehensive answer explores productivity gains, remote working, staff monitoring, and loss of certain jobs. Strong reasoning balances positives (efficiency, access to services) and negatives (reduced empathy, digital divide). Excellent analysis links cultural change to evolving digital skills requirements.','[]',15),(24,7,'State two key requirements of the Health and Safety at Work Act relevant to IT workers. (2 Marks)','A good answer identifies obligations such as providing safe workstations, training, and breaks from display-screen equipment. Excellent responses mention employer duties (risk assessment, eye tests). Weak answers may list unrelated legislation.','[]',16),(25,7,'Describe how the Data Protection Act and GDPR affect software developers. (4 Marks)','Strong answers mention lawful processing, user consent, data minimisation, and eight principles. Well-developed explanations show how programmers must implement secure storage, access control, and rights to erasure. Excellent answers connect compliance to organisational reputation and user trust.','[]',17),(26,7,'Explain the purpose and content of an Acceptable Use Policy (AUP).(5 Marks)','A thorough answer explains that an AUP defines permitted and prohibited activities, communication etiquette, and penalties for breach. Excellent responses note how AUPs protect organisations from misuse and support confidentiality and professional standards. Misconceptions: thinking it is optional or only about internet filters.','[]',18),(27,7,'Evaluate the importance of professional codes of conduct (e.g. BCS, IAP) for software developers. (5 Marks)','High-quality answers evaluate how codes promote ethical practice, competence and public trust. Excellent reasoning contrasts potential benefits (accountability, integrity) with challenges (applying abstract principles in commercial contexts). Students might link to maintaining quality and avoiding conflicts of interest.','[]',19),(28,7,'Discuss how legislation, codes of conduct and security considerations interrelate in digital software development. (6 Marks)','An excellent extended answer integrates multiple strands: Data Protection and Computer Misuse Acts, health and safety, equality law, and professional guidelines. High-level responses analyse how legal compliance, ethical practice and security design (˜secure by design™) reinforce trust and sustainability of software systems.','[]',20),(74,7,'Define algorithm. (1 Mark)','A clear answer states that an algorithm is a precise, step-by-step sequence of instructions used to solve a problem or perform a computation. Stronger responses may note that algorithms can be represented using flowcharts, pseudocode or Python code.','[]',21),(75,7,'What is meant by abstraction in computational thinking?. (1 Mark)','High-quality answers explain that abstraction means removing unnecessary detail to focus only on relevant information in order to simplify complex problems. Weaker responses may confuse it with decomposition.','[]',22),(76,7,'Describe one advantage and one disadvantage of modularisation in programming. (2 Marks)','Strong answers mention that modularisation makes programs easier to test, maintain and reuse but can add complexity when modules need to communicate or share data. A full answer gives both sides with reasoning.','[]',23),(77,7,'Explain how the top-down approach assists in solving software problems. (2 Marks)','A strong response explains that top-down design starts with the overall system and breaks it into smaller sub-systems. Excellent reasoning may refer to writing main functions first and refining details later.','[]',24),(78,7,'Write a short Python function that checks if a number is even or odd and explain how it works. (4 Marks)','Excellent responses produce a correct function using modulus, e.g. `def check_even(n): return \'Even\' if n%2==0 else \'Odd\'`, and explain that `%` gives the remainder of division. Strong reasoning references selection and return statements.','[]',25),(79,7,'Compare the use of flowcharts and pseudocode when expressing algorithms. (4 Marks)','A high-quality answer recognises that flowcharts are visual and easier for beginners, while pseudocode is text-based and closer to actual programming. Excellent students evaluate clarity, structure and audience suitability.','[]',26),(80,7,'Discuss how Boolean operators can simplify complex conditional expressions in Python. (5 Marks)','An excellent answer demonstrates understanding of `and`, `or`, and `not` and shows how combining conditions reduces nested `if` statements. Strong examples and reasoning about readability and logic accuracy improve performance.','[]',27),(81,7,'Explain how debugging supports the development of robust code. (5 Marks)','High-level responses show that debugging identifies and corrects logic or runtime errors, improving reliability. Strong answers describe methods such as print statements or IDE breakpoints and link them to robust and maintainable programs.','[]',28),(82,7,'Evaluate when to use lists, arrays and dictionaries in a Python program. (6 Marks)','Excellent responses compare structures: lists for ordered collections, arrays for numeric efficiency, and dictionaries for key-value pairing. High-quality reasoning includes trade-offs in readability, speed and memory use.','[]',29),(83,7,'Analyse how scope (local vs global variables) affects program reliability. (6 Marks)','A developed answer explains that local scope limits side effects and makes debugging easier, while global scope can cause unintended overwriting. Excellent reasoning may include a Python example showing variable shadowing.','[]',30),(84,7,'Develop and justify an algorithm to validate a users password entry. (8 Marks)','A strong answer outlines checks such as length, inclusion of upper/lowercase, digits, and special characters, then justifies each for security and usability. Excellent students discuss efficiency, data protection and user feedback messages.','[]',31),(85,7,'Explain the purpose of input and output functions in a Python program. (2 Marks)','Good answers note that input collects data from the user and output presents results. A strong response refers to `input()` and `print()` and how they support user interaction.','[]',32),(86,7,'Describe the role of error messages in maintaining robust programs. (4 Marks)','High-quality responses state that meaningful error messages help users and developers understand what went wrong and how to fix it. Excellent answers explain that clear messages prevent frustration and improve maintainability.','[]',33),(87,7,'Analyse the benefits and drawbacks of using loops for iteration. (5 Marks)','Excellent answers identify benefits (automation, reduced code repetition) and drawbacks (risk of infinite loops, logic errors). Strong responses reference Python’s `for` and `while` loops and show understanding of loop control.','[]',34),(88,7,'Explain why algorithm efficiency matters and how it can be measured. (6 Marks)','An excellent answer links efficiency to reduced processing time and memory use, with measurement by counting steps, execution time or comparisons. Stronger responses mention best/worst/average cases and scalability.','[]',35),(89,7,'Define the term artificial intelligence in the context of emerging issues. (2 Marks)','Good answers define AI as systems that perform tasks normally requiring human intelligence, such as decision-making or pattern recognition. Excellent ones note its growing use in automation and software development.','[]',36),(90,7,'Explain how privacy concerns arise from increased use of digital systems. (4 Marks)','A well-developed response refers to data collection, surveillance, and digital footprints. Excellent answers discuss user consent and potential misuse of personal information, linking to legislation such as GDPR.','[]',37),(91,7,'Evaluate the societal effects of generative AI tools. (6 Marks)','High-quality answers consider benefits (productivity, creativity, accessibility) and drawbacks (bias, misinformation, job displacement). Excellent responses analyse how software developers must manage ethical and legal implications.','[]',38),(92,7,'Explain the importance of equality and diversity legislation for software teams. (5 Marks)','Strong answers link equality law to fair treatment, inclusive design, and diverse perspectives improving software quality. Excellent reasoning highlights preventing discrimination and encouraging accessible digital solutions.','[]',39),(93,7,'Discuss how ethical, legal and environmental factors influence digital software development. (8 Marks)','An outstanding answer integrates multiple aspects: data protection, intellectual property, accessibility, sustainability and AI ethics. High-quality reasoning shows balanced evaluation of responsibilities developers face when designing modern systems.','[]',40),(94,7,'What is meant by decomposition?. (1 Mark)','A clear answer states that decomposition is the process of breaking a large or complex problem into smaller, more manageable sub-problems. Stronger answers may mention that this supports modular coding and easier debugging.','[]',41),(95,7,'Define the term variable in programming. (1 Mark)','High-quality responses identify a variable as a named storage location that holds a value which can change during program execution. Excellent ones may note that it has a data type and scope.','[]',42),(96,7,'Explain why comments are used in Python programs. (2 Marks)','Good answers say that comments describe the purpose of code and aid readability. Stronger responses explain that they help others maintain, debug or extend a program without altering behaviour.','[]',43),(97,7,'Describe how pattern recognition assists problem solving. (2 Marks)','An effective answer shows that pattern recognition identifies similarities between problems, allowing reuse of prior solutions. Excellent responses include an example such as recognising repeated validation logic.','[]',44),(98,7,'Explain with an example how selection is implemented in Python. (4 Marks)','A strong answer uses `if`, `elif`, and `else` to demonstrate conditional logic. Example: checking age to print different messages. Excellent responses discuss Boolean conditions and indentation rules.','[]',45),(99,7,'Compare count-controlled and condition-controlled loops. (4 Marks)','High-quality responses explain that count-controlled loops run a fixed number of times (`for`), whereas condition-controlled loops (`while`) repeat until a condition changes. Excellent answers evaluate which is safer or more efficient in context.','[]',46),(100,7,'Discuss how validation and verification work together to improve data quality. (5 Marks)','Excellent answers define validation as checking data meets criteria and verification as confirming accuracy (e.g., double entry). Strong reasoning notes both are needed to ensure reliable input and prevent downstream errors.','[]',47),(101,7,'Explain how debugging and testing differ during software development. (5 Marks)','Strong answers note debugging finds and fixes specific errors while testing checks the system meets requirements. Excellent reasoning may include that debugging is reactive, testing is planned and proactive.','[]',48),(102,7,'Evaluate the use of pseudocode when designing algorithms. (6 Marks)','High-quality answers explain pseudocode bridges natural language and code, improving clarity and team understanding. Excellent answers analyse drawbacks such as inconsistent syntax and lack of executability.','[]',49),(103,7,'Analyse the effect of using inefficient algorithms on software performance. (6 Marks)','Excellent answers link inefficiency to longer execution times, higher resource usage and poorer user experience. Strong responses give examples like bubble sort vs merge sort and mention scalability concerns.','[]',50),(104,7,'Design an algorithm (in flowchart or pseudocode) to find the average of a list of exam scores and justify your approach. (8 Marks)','Excellent answers include input, total accumulation, count, division and output steps. Justifications mention correctness, readability, and possible validation for empty lists or invalid data.','[]',51),(105,7,'Describe the difference between integers and floats in Python. (2 Marks)','A good answer explains integers are whole numbers and floats represent real numbers with decimals. Strong answers may give examples such as `x=3` vs `y=3.14`.','[]',52),(106,7,'Explain why consistent naming conventions improve collaboration in a team project. (4 Marks)','High-quality responses show that standard names reduce confusion, make version control cleaner and help debugging. Excellent reasoning mentions conventions like snake_case or camelCase.','[]',53),(107,7,'Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of open-source software libraries. (5 Marks)','Strong answers highlight benefits (free access, community support, transparency) and drawbacks (variable quality, lack of official support). Excellent evaluations balance these in the context of professional use.','[]',54),(108,7,'Evaluate the role of testing methods such as unit and integration testing. (6 Marks)','Excellent answers differentiate that unit testing verifies individual components, while integration testing checks they work together. Strong analysis notes early detection of errors and reduced maintenance costs.','[]',55),(109,7,'State two examples of emerging digital technologies affecting software design. (2 Marks)','Good answers may mention AI, quantum computing, IoT, blockchain or virtual reality. Stronger ones pick relevant examples and show awareness of their impact on developers.','[]',56),(110,7,'Explain how automation affects employment in the digital sector. (4 Marks)','High-quality responses outline that automation can increase efficiency but reduce certain roles. Excellent answers balance this with new job creation in maintenance and AI oversight.','[]',57),(111,7,'Analyse how accessibility laws influence software interface design. (5 Marks)','Excellent responses link legislation to inclusive design, requiring features such as alt text, keyboard navigation and colour-contrast compliance. Strong analysis discusses benefits for users and compliance risk for organisations.','[]',58),(112,7,'Evaluate the importance of professional development for digital professionals. (6 Marks)','High-quality answers argue that continued learning maintains competence and adaptability in fast-changing technologies. Excellent ones connect it to ethics, employability and quality assurance.','[]',59),(113,7,'Discuss how multiple laws and guidelines (Data Protection, Computer Misuse, Equality, Health & Safety) together shape responsible software practice. (8 Marks)','An excellent extended response integrates how these laws ensure secure, fair and safe digital systems. High-level reasoning links compliance to reputation, user trust and sustainable professional behaviour.','[]',60),(114,11,'Explain what is meant by computational thinking. (2 Marks)','Good answers define computational thinking as the process of breaking problems into logical, structured steps that a computer can follow. Strong answers mention decomposition, pattern recognition, abstraction and algorithm design.','[]',1),(115,11,'Describe what an algorithm is and its purpose in problem solving. (2 Marks)','Answers identify an algorithm as a step-by-step set of instructions for solving a problem or performing a task. Better responses note its role in ensuring solutions are repeatable and logical.','[]',2),(116,11,'State two benefits of using flowcharts to represent an algorithm. (2 Marks)','Strong answers mention clarity, easy debugging or communication of logic between developers. Common misconceptions include treating flowcharts as code rather than a planning tool.','[]',3),(117,11,'Explain what is meant by decomposition in computational thinking. (2 Marks)','Good answers state that decomposition breaks complex problems into smaller, manageable parts. High-quality responses link this to modular programming or troubleshooting.','[]',4),(118,11,'Identify two advantages of using abstraction in problem solving. (2 Marks)','Strong responses mention simplification of complex systems and focusing on relevant details. Misconceptions include removing essential details entirely.','[]',5),(119,11,'Describe what pattern recognition involves. (2 Marks)','Good answers describe finding similarities or trends within data or problems to inform solutions. Better responses provide an example such as identifying repeated error logs.','[]',6),(120,11,'Explain the difference between syntax errors and logic errors. (2 Marks)','Strong responses state that syntax errors break code execution due to rule violations, while logic errors produce incorrect results despite running.','[]',7),(121,11,'Identify two key stages of the problem-solving process. (2 Marks)','Expected responses include defining the problem, designing a solution, implementing, testing or evaluating. Strong answers link stages together coherently.','[]',8),(122,11,'Define the term ‘trace table’. (2 Marks)','Good answers state it is a tool used to record variable changes during algorithm testing. High-quality responses may include its role in debugging.','[]',9),(123,11,'Explain why testing is an important part of the problem-solving process. (2 Marks)','Strong answers state it verifies correctness and reliability. Better responses note it ensures requirements are met and identifies logical or runtime errors.','[]',10),(124,11,'Explain how hardware and software components interact within a digital system. (4 Marks)','Good answers describe how hardware executes instructions provided by software. Strong responses include examples such as OS controlling memory or drivers managing devices.','[]',11),(125,11,'Describe how preventative maintenance helps digital systems remain reliable. (4 Marks)','Strong answers include tasks such as updates, backups, and cleaning hardware. Weaker answers may only mention repair after failure.','[]',12),(126,11,'Explain the purpose of digital support within an organisation. (4 Marks)','Good answers state it maintains system functionality, resolves incidents and improves user productivity. Stronger responses link to SLAs and customer satisfaction.','[]',13),(127,11,'Discuss the importance of documentation in digital support. (4 Marks)','High-quality responses explain that documentation supports consistent troubleshooting and knowledge sharing. Misconceptions may confuse it with user manuals only.','[]',14),(128,11,'Describe the steps a technician might take to diagnose a network connectivity issue. (4 Marks)','Expected points include checking physical connections, IP configuration, ping testing, and escalation. Better answers mention systematic logging.','[]',15),(129,11,'Explain the difference between corrective and adaptive maintenance. (4 Marks)','Strong responses state that corrective fixes faults while adaptive changes systems for new environments. Misconceptions may confuse adaptive with preventive.','[]',16),(130,11,'Describe how monitoring tools can support proactive maintenance. (4 Marks)','Good answers note they detect issues before failure, through alerts on CPU, memory or network traffic. Strong responses link to reduced downtime.','[]',17),(131,11,'Explain how Service Level Agreements (SLAs) affect digital support operations. (4 Marks)','High-quality answers mention performance targets, response times and accountability. Weak responses may define SLA but not its impact.','[]',18),(132,11,'Discuss how virtualisation can simplify system management. (4 Marks)','Good answers mention resource efficiency, easier backups, and testing environments. Stronger answers address cost savings and isolation.','[]',19),(133,11,'Describe how remote support technologies benefit both users and organisations. (4 Marks)','Good answers include faster resolution and reduced travel costs. Strong responses discuss security and user consent.','[]',20),(134,11,'Discuss how data quality impacts decision making in organisations. (6 Marks)','Strong responses explain that poor-quality data leads to incorrect conclusions and wasted resources. Excellent answers mention accuracy, completeness and timeliness dimensions.','[]',21),(135,11,'Explain the difference between structured and unstructured data with examples. (6 Marks)','Good answers define structured data as organised in fixed fields (e.g., spreadsheets) and unstructured as free-form (e.g., emails, images). Strong responses explain storage and analysis implications.','[]',22),(136,11,'Evaluate the importance of data validation during data entry. (6 Marks)','High-quality responses discuss how validation ensures accuracy and reduces processing errors. Stronger answers describe types such as range, presence, and format checks.','[]',23),(137,11,'Discuss how databases can enforce data integrity. (6 Marks)','Good answers mention primary and foreign keys, constraints, and referential integrity. Strong answers explain prevention of duplication and inconsistent records.','[]',24),(138,11,'Explain how data visualisation supports understanding of complex datasets. (6 Marks)','Strong answers describe using charts, dashboards, and heatmaps to reveal trends. Better responses emphasise audience needs and misrepresentation risks.','[]',25),(139,11,'Discuss the ethical considerations when collecting and storing personal data. (8 Marks)','Strong responses cover privacy, consent, data minimisation, and compliance with GDPR. Excellent answers weigh business needs against individual rights.','[]',26),(140,11,'Analyse the advantages and disadvantages of cloud storage for sensitive data. (8 Marks)','High-quality responses discuss scalability and accessibility benefits versus risks like data breaches and compliance challenges.','[]',27),(141,11,'Evaluate how data analytics tools can improve organisational efficiency. (8 Marks)','Good answers identify predictive insights and process optimisation. Stronger responses consider investment cost and data literacy needs.','[]',28),(142,11,'Discuss the challenges of integrating data from multiple sources. (8 Marks)','Strong responses highlight compatibility, format conversion, and data quality issues. Excellent answers mention ETL processes and metadata management.','[]',29),(143,11,'Assess how backup strategies contribute to data security and business continuity. (8 Marks)','Good answers explain regular backups reduce data loss. Strong responses include types (full, incremental, differential) and offsite storage importance.','[]',30),(144,11,'Define the term ‘logic gate’ and state one example. (2 Marks)','Good answers define logic gates as electronic components that perform logical operations on inputs to produce a single output. Examples include AND, OR, and NOT gates.','[]',31),(145,11,'Explain what is meant by iteration in problem solving. (2 Marks)','Strong responses state that iteration involves repeating a set of instructions until a condition is met. Good answers may mention loops or refining algorithms.','[]',32),(146,11,'State one difference between linear and binary search algorithms. (2 Marks)','Expected answers include that linear search checks each item in order, while binary search divides data in half repeatedly. Strong responses mention sorted data requirements.','[]',33),(147,11,'Identify two benefits of writing pseudocode before coding. (2 Marks)','Good answers include easier planning, reduced syntax errors, and improved understanding of algorithm structure.','[]',34),(148,11,'Explain what is meant by modular programming. (2 Marks)','Strong responses state that modular programming divides programs into independent sections or modules for easier debugging and maintenance.',